post "ad_15_krishna_flute" "A 12-second simple mini-film. Young Krishna — a beautiful boy of about 12, blue-tinted skin, long dark curly hair, peacock feather tucked behind one ear, yellow silk dhoti tied at the waist, simple gold jewelry, holding a wooden bansuri (flute) — plays his flute at the edge of the Yamuna river at golden hour. Setting: Vrindavan riverbank, soft amber light, river water glowing, a few peacocks nearby, gentle reeds. Scene 1 (6s, mid close): Krishna stands cross-legged leaning against a tree, brings the flute to his lips, begins to play. Soft smile, eyes closed. Camera 6-8 ft, three-quarter angle, soft golden rim light. Mood: peace, devotion. Scene 2 (6s, response wide): Pulled-back shot — two peacocks fan their tails in response, a calf turns its head toward him, the river ripples gently. Krishna still playing in the corner of the frame. Camera 20-25 ft, wide. Mood: enchantment, harmony. Consistency: SAME Krishna figure both scenes; SAME Vrindavan riverbank; warm-gold palette." 12

post "ad_16_krishna_butter" "A 12-second simple mini-film. Young Krishna as a small toddler of about 4 — chubby blue-tinted child, large bright eyes, curly hair with one peacock feather, tiny yellow dhoti, anklets, soft baby cheeks — sneaks butter from a clay pot in a humble Vrindavan kitchen. Setting: warm clay-walled village kitchen, terracotta pots, a low wooden stool, soft window light. Scene 1 (6s, action close): Krishna stands on his tiptoes on a wooden stool, reaches both hands into a clay pot of fresh white butter (makhan), grins mischievously as he scoops out a handful, smears some on his cheek. Camera 4-5 ft, three-quarter. Mood: playful, adorable. Scene 2 (6s, caught wide): His mother Yashoda enters from the doorway — gentle woman in a simple sari, surprised then smiling expression. Krishna looks at her with butter-covered fingers, frozen in the act, eyes wide and innocent. Camera 12-15 ft, wide. Mood: love, mischief, warmth. Consistency: SAME toddler Krishna both scenes; SAME kitchen; warm golden-brown palette." 12

post "ad_17_krishna_cows" "A 12-second simple mini-film. Young Krishna — a beautiful boy of about 12, blue-tinted skin, long dark curly hair, peacock feather behind one ear, yellow silk dhoti, simple gold jewelry, a long shepherd's stick — leads a herd of cows through the meadows of Vrindavan at sunset. Setting: open green meadow, low golden sun, scattered banyan trees, a small group of brown-and-white cows with calves. Scene 1 (6s, wide tracking): Krishna walks barefoot through the meadow with a small herd of about 8 cows following him. He turns once and looks at a calf trailing behind, smiles. Camera 25-30 ft, slow tracking. Mood: pastoral, joyful, golden. Scene 2 (6s, close moment): Tight medium shot — Krishna kneels beside the trailing calf and gently strokes its head. The calf nuzzles his hand. Soft sunset rim light. Camera 5-6 ft. Mood: tender, divine simplicity. Consistency: SAME Krishna figure both scenes; SAME meadow; warm golden-green palette." 12

# ── Saint Dnyaneshwar ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
post "ad_18_dnyaneshwar_newasa" "A 30-second mini-film. Saint Dnyaneshwar — a young Marathi saint-poet of about 15, slim youthful frame, gentle radiant face, long dark hair tied loosely, simple ochre dhoti and shawl, rudraksha mala, calm luminous eyes — dictates the Dnyaneshwari (his Marathi commentary on the Bhagavad Gita) at Newasa, Maharashtra, in the year 1290 CE. Setting: a large gnarled banyan tree at the edge of a quiet village, late-afternoon golden light, a small group of scribes seated in a semicircle taking down his words on palm-leaf manuscripts with iron styluses. Scene 1 (6s, wide establishing): The banyan tree with its aerial roots, a stone platform beneath, Dnyaneshwar seated on the platform, six scribes around him in a half-circle. Indian village backdrop. Camera 30-40 ft, slow push-in. Mood: serene scholarship. Scene 2 (6s, dictation mid): Dnyaneshwar speaks gently with one hand raised in a teaching gesture, eyes half-closed, lips moving softly. Camera 8-10 ft, three-quarter. Mood: inspired transmission. Scene 3 (6s, scribe close): Tight close-up of an older scribe's hands — wrinkled fingers gripping an iron stylus, etching Marathi script onto a long narrow palm-leaf. Inkwell beside him. Camera 2-3 ft, macro feel. Mood: preservation, devotion. Scene 4 (6s, listener faces): Slow pan across the faces of three scribes — each one rapt, eyes shining, one with a single tear, expressions of receiving sacred knowledge. Camera 5 ft, side dolly. Mood: reverence. Scene 5 (6s, final wide): Dnyaneshwar finishes speaking, lowers his hand, smiles gently. The scribes bow their heads. Golden hour light filters through banyan leaves above them. Camera 20-30 ft, wide. Mood: completion, legacy. Consistency: SAME Dnyaneshwar figure across all five scenes; SAME banyan tree and stone platform setting; same six scribes; warm amber-green palette." 30

post "ad_19_dnyaneshwar_buffalo" "A 24-second mini-film depicting the famous miracle of Saint Dnyaneshwar making a buffalo recite the Vedas. Saint Dnyaneshwar — a young saint-poet of about 14, slim radiant frame, gentle face, long dark hair tied loosely, simple ochre dhoti and shawl, rudraksha mala, calm eyes — stands at the edge of a Maharashtrian village street where a group of orthodox brahmin priests in white dhotis with shaved heads have been mocking him and his siblings. A large dark water-buffalo stands placidly nearby chewing grass. Scene 1 (6s, confrontation wide): The brahmins gesturing dismissively at Dnyaneshwar and his siblings (a girl and two boys, all young, in simple clothing). The buffalo in the background. Village backdrop. Camera 20-25 ft, wide. Mood: tension. Scene 2 (6s, decision mid): Dnyaneshwar's face — calm and unprovoked. He turns slowly and walks toward the buffalo, gently placing his right hand on its forehead. Camera 8 ft, three-quarter. Mood: quiet conviction. Scene 3 (6s, miracle close): Tight close-up of the buffalo's face — large dark eyes blink, then its mouth opens and clear Sanskrit verses emanate (visualize as faint golden light around its mouth, sound waves). Dnyaneshwar's hand still on its forehead. Camera 3 ft. Mood: wonder. Scene 4 (6s, awe wide): The brahmins fall to their knees, hands joined in pranam, expressions of shock and humility. Dnyaneshwar lowers his hand and steps back, calm and unchanged. The buffalo silent again, chewing grass. Camera 20-25 ft, wide. Mood: humility, divine demonstration. Consistency: SAME Dnyaneshwar figure across all four scenes; SAME buffalo; SAME village street; warm earth-tone palette." 24

post "ad_20_dnyaneshwar_samadhi" "A 24-second mini-film of Saint Dnyaneshwar entering Sanjeevan Samadhi (voluntary living entombment) at Alandi at age 21 in 1296 CE. Dnyaneshwar — slim radiant young saint, gentle luminous face, long dark hair, simple ochre dhoti and shawl, rudraksha mala — descends into an underground stone chamber to enter a state of conscious eternal meditation. Setting: the Alandi sacred ground beside the Indrayani river, devotees and his siblings gathered around a small stone-lined underground chamber with a square opening at ground level. Soft early-morning light, marigold petals scattered. Scene 1 (6s, gathering wide): A small gathered crowd in respectful silence — including his three siblings (Nivritti, Sopan, Muktabai) standing nearest the chamber opening. Dnyaneshwar stands at the edge of the opening, calm and ready. Camera 25-30 ft, wide. Mood: solemn, sacred. Scene 2 (6s, farewell close): Tight close-up of Dnyaneshwar's face — peaceful smile, eyes shining, looking once at his siblings. His sister Muktabai's face beside him with a single tear but accepting. Camera 4-5 ft. Mood: tender farewell, acceptance. Scene 3 (6s, descent mid): Dnyaneshwar lowers himself gracefully into the underground stone chamber, seats himself cross-legged in padmasana, hands resting on knees in chin mudra, eyes closed. The chamber stone visible around him. Camera 8-10 ft, looking down into the chamber. Mood: surrender, eternity. Scene 4 (6s, sealing wide): A large flat stone slab is gently lowered over the opening by the devotees, sealing the chamber. Marigold petals scattered on top. The siblings bow. Soft golden light. Camera 15-20 ft, wide. Mood: completion, eternal presence. Consistency: SAME Dnyaneshwar figure across all four scenes; SAME chamber and Alandi setting; same siblings; warm gold-ochre palette." 24

